import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# 1. Load Dataset
df = pd.read_csv("Superstore.csv", encoding="latin1")

# 2. Display Dataset Information
print("First 5 Records:")
print(df.head())

print("\nDataset Shape:")
print(df.shape)

print("\nColumn Names:")
print(df.columns.tolist())

print("\nDataset Information:")
df.info()

print("\nMissing Values:")
print(df.isnull().sum())

# 3. Clean Column Names
df.columns = (
    df.columns
    .str.strip()
    .str.lower()
    .str.replace(" ", "_")
)

print("\nCleaned Column Names:")
print(df.columns.tolist())

# 4. Calculate Total Sales by Region
region_sales = df.groupby("region")["sales"].sum()

print("\nTotal Sales by Region:")
print(region_sales)

# 5. Bar Chart
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
region_sales.plot(kind="bar")
plt.title("Total Sales by Region")
plt.xlabel("Region")
plt.ylabel("Sales")
plt.xticks(rotation=0)
plt.show()

# 6. Pie Chart
plt.figure(figsize=(7, 7))
region_sales.plot(kind="pie", autopct="%1.1f%%")
plt.title("Sales Distribution by Region")
plt.ylabel("")
plt.show()

# 7. Sort Regions by Sales
top_regions = region_sales.sort_values(ascending=False)

print("\nTop Performing Regions:")
print(top_regions)

# 8. Sales by Region and Category
region_category_sales = df.groupby(
    ["region", "category"]
)["sales"].sum()

print("\nSales by Region and Category:")
print(region_category_sales)

# 9. Create Pivot Table
pivot_data = df.pivot_table(
    values="sales",
    index="region",
    columns="category",
    aggfunc="sum",
    fill_value=0
)

print("\nRegion and Category Sales:")
print(pivot_data)

# 10. Display Grouped Bar Chart
pivot_data.plot(kind="bar", figsize=(10, 6))

plt.title("Sales by Region and Category")
plt.xlabel("Region")
plt.ylabel("Sales")
plt.xticks(rotation=0)
plt.legend(title="Category")
plt.show()





Import libraries
import pandas as pdimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt



- import pandas as pd – Imports Pandas for data analysis.
- import matplotlib.pyplot as plt – Imports Matplotlib for data visualization.
- pd and plt are aliases (short names).
2. Load dataset
df = pd.read_csv("Superstore.csv", encoding="latin1")



- pd.read_csv() – Reads the CSV file.
- "Superstore.csv" – Name of the dataset.
- encoding="latin1" – Helps read special characters in the CSV file.
- df – Stores the dataset in DataFrame format.
3. Display dataset information
print("First 5 Records:")print(df.head())



- print() – Displays output.
- df.head() – Displays the first five rows of the dataset.
print("\nDataset Shape:")print(df.shape)



- \n – Starts a new line.
- df.shape – Displays the number of rows and columns.
print("\nColumn Names:")print(df.columns.tolist())



- df.columns – Displays all column names.
- .tolist() – Converts column names into a list.
print("\nDataset Information:")df.info()



- df.info() – Displays column names, data types and non-null values.
print("\nMissing Values:")print(df.isnull().sum())



- df.isnull() – Checks missing values.
- .sum() – Counts missing values in each column.
Purpose: To understand the dataset before performing analysis.
4. Clean column names
df.columns = (    df.columns    .str.strip()    .str.lower()    .str.replace(" ", "_"))



Line-by-line explanation:
- df.columns – Selects all column names.
- .str.strip() – Removes extra spaces from the beginning and end.
- .str.lower() – Converts column names into lowercase.
- .str.replace(" ", "_") – Replaces spaces with underscores.
- df.columns = – Assigns the cleaned column names back to the DataFrame.
Example:
Original column	Cleaned column
Region	region
Sales	sales
Order Date	order_date
Customer Name	customer_name

print("\nCleaned Column Names:")print(df.columns.tolist())



- Displays the cleaned column names.

Purpose: To make column names simple and easy to use in Python.
5. Calculate total sales by region
region_sales = df.groupby("region")["sales"].sum()print("\nTotal Sales by Region:")print(region_sales)



Explanation:
- df.groupby("region") – Groups records according to region.
- ["sales"] – Selects the sales column.
- .sum() – Calculates the total sales of each region.
- region_sales – Stores the calculated result.
- print() – Displays total sales by region.
Example output:
region
Central     501239
East        678781
South       391722
West        725458


These are illustrative values; actual output depends on your dataset.

Purpose: To find which region generates the highest total sales.
6. Bar chart
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))region_sales.plot(kind="bar")plt.title("Total Sales by Region")plt.xlabel("Region")plt.ylabel("Sales")plt.xticks(rotation=0)plt.show()



Explanation:
- plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5)) – Creates a graph with width 8 and height 5.
- region_sales.plot(kind="bar") – Creates a bar chart using regional sales.
- plt.title() – Adds the graph title.
- plt.xlabel() – Labels the X-axis as Region.
- plt.ylabel() – Labels the Y-axis as Sales.
- plt.xticks(rotation=0) – Keeps region names horizontal.
- plt.show() – Displays the graph.
Purpose: To compare total sales across different regions.
7. Pie chart
plt.figure(figsize=(7, 7))region_sales.plot(kind="pie", autopct="%1.1f%%")plt.title("Sales Distribution by Region")plt.ylabel("")plt.show()



Explanation:
- plt.figure(figsize=(7, 7)) – Creates a square figure.
- region_sales.plot(kind="pie") – Creates a pie chart.
- autopct="%1.1f%%" – Displays percentage values up to one decimal place.
- plt.title() – Adds the graph title.
- plt.ylabel("") – Removes the default Y-axis label.
- plt.show() – Displays the pie chart.
Purpose: To show the percentage contribution of each region to total sales.
8. Sort regions by sales
top_regions = region_sales.sort_values(ascending=False)print("\nTop Performing Regions:")print(top_regions)



Explanation:
- region_sales – Contains total sales of each region.
- .sort_values() – Sorts the sales values.
- ascending=False – Sorts values in descending order (highest to lowest).
- top_regions – Stores the sorted result.
- print() – Displays the regions according to sales performance.
Purpose: To identify the highest and lowest performing regions.
9. Sales by region and category
region_category_sales = df.groupby(    ["region", "category"])["sales"].sum()print("\nSales by Region and Category:")print(region_category_sales)



Explanation:
- df.groupby(["region", "category"]) – Groups data using two columns: region and category.
- ["sales"] – Selects the sales column.
- .sum() – Calculates total sales for each region-category combination.
- region_category_sales – Stores the result.
- print() – Displays the aggregated sales.
Purpose: To understand how much each product category contributes to sales in every region.

For example, it shows:
- Central region – Furniture sales.
- Central region – Office Supplies sales.
- Central region – Technology sales.
- The same breakdown for other regions.
10. Create pivot table
pivot_data = df.pivot_table(    values="sales",    index="region",    columns="category",    aggfunc="sum",    fill_value=0)



Explanation:
- df.pivot_table() – Creates a summarized table.
- values="sales" – Uses the sales column for calculations.
- index="region" – Displays regions as rows.
- columns="category" – Displays categories as columns.
- aggfunc="sum" – Calculates the total sales.
- fill_value=0 – Replaces missing combinations with zero.
- pivot_data – Stores the pivot table.
print("\nRegion and Category Sales:")print(pivot_data)



- Displays the summarized sales table.

Example format:
Region	Furniture	Office Supplies	Technology
Central	10000	12000	15000
East	14000	16000	18000
South	9000	11000	13000
West	17000	19000	22000

Illustrative values only.

Purpose: To summarize sales for each category and region in a tabular format.
11. Display grouped bar chart
pivot_data.plot(kind="bar", figsize=(10, 6))plt.title("Sales by Region and Category")plt.xlabel("Region")plt.ylabel("Sales")plt.xticks(rotation=0)plt.legend(title="Category")plt.show()



Explanation:
- pivot_data.plot(kind="bar") – Creates a grouped bar chart from the pivot table.
- figsize=(10, 6) – Sets graph size.
- plt.title() – Adds the graph title.
- plt.xlabel() – Labels the X-axis.
- plt.ylabel() – Labels the Y-axis.
- plt.xticks(rotation=0) – Keeps region names horizontal.
- plt.legend(title="Category") – Displays category names in the legend.
- plt.show() – Displays the graph.
Purpose: To compare Furniture, Office Supplies and Technology sales across different regions.

